  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "DRIFT MONITORING AND TRAINING SAFETY FIXES. Two defects, both reproduced before and after the fix. SUSTAINED DRIFT NOW ALERTS FOR AS LONG AS IT LASTS: the adaptive baseline (mean + 2 sigma over the last 30 windows) recorded every score including the drifted ones, so a sustained shift re-normalised itself into the new normal - the mean climbed, sigma collapsed, and the threshold converged on the drifted score until the score no longer exceeded its own threshold. At the shipped defaults (3 consecutive windows, 30-window baseline, 2 sigma) a sustained shift fired exactly one alert in 32 windows, and none at all when it began within a few windows of a reference capture, because a baseline too small to compute a threshold from was read as \"the adaptive gate says no\" rather than \"the gate has no opinion\". Operators saw Critical in GET /v1/drift with no pending retraining trigger. The baseline now learns from every window EXCEPT the critical ones, and abstains while it holds fewer than AdaptiveMinimumSamples scores (new setting, default 5, capped by the baseline window size) instead of vetoing the static threshold - new ThresholdVerdict case StaticWhileWarmingUp, and the consecutive-window policy counts a verdict through ThresholdVerdict.isExceedance. Measured at the defaults: the same sustained shift now fires every 3 windows whether it starts after a long healthy run, two windows after a reference capture, or in the very first analysed window; an isolated spike still fires nothing; and a merely noisy signal that crosses the static line in 212 of 300 windows without ever shifting stays at 2 alerts. Excluding every static exceedance from the baseline instead of only the critical ones was measured and rejected - it truncates the baseline to the quiet tail and turns that same noisy signal into 47 alerts - so Warning-level windows stay in the baseline as part of how noisy a signal normally is. fuuga decide train REFUSES TO OVERWRITE ITS OWN CHECKPOINT: a decision model is written flat into --output under the same model_weights.dat and checkpoint.json names it read from --checkpoint, so pointing both at one directory replaced the donor's weights with the fine-tuned encoder and re-tagged its metadata Bidirectional - the generative checkpoint was gone, and fuuga serve refuses a bidirectional checkpoint as the served model. The command now exits 1 with an explanation before any work happens (DecisionHeads.wouldOverwriteCheckpoint); the flat layout is unchanged, since a decision model directory has to stay loadable as --decision-model. TESTS: every alerting test shrank the configuration to 1-2 consecutive windows, a 3-5 window baseline and 1 sigma, so the shipped numbers were never exercised - a new section runs the defaults, covering sustained drift, drift before the baseline warms, the noisy-signal false-positive case, isolated spikes, and that critical windows never enter the baseline. PREVIOUS (1.0.24) - DID THE RE-TRAINED MODEL ACTUALLY GET BETTER? A/B AGAINST RECORDED OUTCOMES (TypedDecisions): pair two prediction dumps by (example, question), score both against the labels a business already records as a by-product of operating, and report accuracy, NLL, Brier, ECE/MCE, reliability bins and selective accuracy at each act-threshold side by side, with a paired McNemar exact test, a Wilson interval on the win share and a per-question breakdown; the verdict is BETTER, WORSE, INDISTINGUISHABLE or NO EVIDENCE. fuuga decide compare keeps the existing fidelity gate when called without --data and switches to the measured A/B when --data supplies labelled fixtures; exit 3 means a measured regression only, wiring errors exit 1. TYPED LLM-AS-JUDGE (JudgeArena, fuuga decide judge): judge two models' free-text answers pairwise as a typed choice over A, B or tie, both presentation orders averaged so position bias is measured and a pair whose orders disagree is a tie, never a win; win rate with a Wilson interval and an exact-test p-value, per-category breakdown, gating, a JSONL audit trail, and the judge's own agreement and calibration against any human labels. EXAMPLE: examples/retrain-verification.fsx verifies a re-trained donor model end to end offline on CPU in about a minute. PREVIOUS (1.0.23) - TYPED DECISIONS (System 1, the Laya / Jev pattern): typed choice, score and noul questions answered as a probability for every named option, zero-shot via option-tag log-probs or natively from bilinear decision heads on a bidirectional Fuuga checkpoint (fuuga decide train), temperature scaling per question kind and option-count bucket, calibration metrics, an act-or-escalate gate, POST /v1/decide, and the drift monitor (fuuga drift, --drift-state) that files retraining triggers carrying an approve-or-reject brief and never retrains automatically.",
